; Stage 16 (design/stage16-ergonomics/repl-jit-symbol-precedence.md): the REPL
; used to refuse `(import-use nucleus.node)` with `Duplicate definition of symbol
; 'alloc-node'`. The compiler attached its own process-symbol generator to the
; MAIN JITDylib, so the first macro that referenced a node-runtime symbol made
; ORC reflect the host's copy INTO main, and the name was then taken for the
; rest of the session. LLJIT already reflects process symbols through its own
; "<Process Symbols>" JITDylib, linked LAST, so removing the redundant generator
; restores the documented precedence: a module's own definition wins.
;
; 1. quote works at the prompt (it lowers to a runtime intern-symbol call)
'a
'(a b)
; 2. `case` expands to `cond`. Expanding it HERE materializes its JIT module
;    while the only intern-symbol in the session is the host compiler's, which
;    binds it to that copy permanently (ORC materialization is lazy and
;    one-shot). §5 below re-checks it after the import.
(case 1 1 101 999)
; 3. the node runtime imports — this is the form the whole item was about.
;    It pulls lib/nucleus/arena.nuc in with it, so the explicit import dedups.
(import-use nucleus.node)
(import-use nucleus.arena)
; 4. and its functions are callable afterwards
(node-len '(a b c))
(node-len '())
; 5. the already-materialized macro still works across the import
(case 2 2 202 999)
; 6. RESIDUAL, pinned deliberately (§5 of the design doc): a macro first
;    expanded AFTER the import binds intern-symbol to the SESSION's copy of
;    lib/nucleus/node.nuc, whose intern table is a second table. `emit-node` dispatches
;    special forms by POINTER identity on the interned head symbol
;    (conventions.md, "Symbol nodes are interned singletons"), so a special-form
;    head minted from that second table is not recognised. It fails LOUDLY.
;    When the follow-up item lands, this stops erroring and this fixture must be
;    updated rather than deleted.
(defmacro hr-pick (a) `(cond (> ~a 5) 111 true 222))
(hr-pick 9)
; 7. ...while a macro whose expansion has no special-form head is unaffected,
;    because a function head is resolved by SPELLING, not by pointer identity
(defmacro hr-add (a b) `(+ ~a ~b))
(hr-add 20 22)
